                <div class="comparison-plaintext sr-only" aria-hidden="true">
                    <h3>{% t %}LANBucket feature comparison (plain-text summary){% endt %}</h3>
                    <p>{% t %}This is a plain-text version of the comparison table above, comparing LANBucket against DC++, D-LAN, LocalSend, SMB shares, cloud storage, Steam local sharing, and a USB flash drive. Each entry lists whether the tool offers the feature (Yes, Partial, or No) and why.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>No setup or configuration.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: No &mdash; DC++ makes you add a hub to connect to and set up your share list before you can use it. D-LAN: Partial &mdash; D-LAN finds peers automatically, but you still choose which folders to share and where downloads go. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: No &mdash; SMB shares require enabling sharing, setting permissions, and mapping drives, often with admin rights. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; you must create an account, install a client, and let it sync before sharing anything. Steam Local Sharing: Partial &mdash; you need Steam installed and signed in, plus the relevant sharing setting enabled. USB Flash Drive: No &mdash; the drive has to be formatted for your devices and physically plugged into each one.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>No account or sign-in required.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: Yes. D-LAN: Yes. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: No &mdash; SMB needs valid user credentials or a network account to authenticate access. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; cloud storage requires creating and signing into an account before you can transfer files. Steam Local Sharing: No &mdash; you must have a Steam account and be signed in to use any Steam feature. USB Flash Drive: Yes.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Distributed (many-to-many) transfers.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: Partial &mdash; DC++ can pull a file from several peers at once, but only once the other peer has completed the download; LANBucket allows downloading from peers who don't have the complete file. D-LAN: Yes. LocalSend: No &mdash; LocalSend sends directly from one device to another, one transfer at a time. SMB Share: No &mdash; an SMB share is served by a single host, so everyone downloads from that one machine. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; transfers go through the provider's servers rather than directly between many local peers. Steam Local Sharing: No &mdash; content comes from a single host, not many peers sharing the load. USB Flash Drive: No &mdash; a flash drive is copied to one machine at a time by hand.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Automatic peer discovery.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: No &mdash; there's no auto-discovery; you have to know and enter a hub address to find others. D-LAN: Yes. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: No &mdash; you need the host name or IP address of the share to connect to it. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; there are no local peers to find; everything routes through the provider's servers. Steam Local Sharing: Yes. USB Flash Drive: No &mdash; a flash drive has no networking, so there's nothing to discover.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Best in class performance.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: No &mdash; DC++ only downloads from peers that already have the complete file, so when everyone grabs a new file at once, it all comes from the original sharer. D-LAN: Partial &mdash; D-LAN is much faster than traditional file shares, but in testing we have measured LANBucket as much as ~2x faster than D-LAN. LocalSend: No &mdash; every copy comes straight from the sending device, so its network link is the bottleneck when several people need the file. SMB Share: No &mdash; everyone downloads from the one PC hosting the share, so its network link is the bottleneck. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; every download comes over your internet connection, which is usually much slower than your local network. Steam Local Sharing: No &mdash; content comes from a single host PC, so its network link is the bottleneck. USB Flash Drive: No &mdash; the drive has to be copied to each machine one at a time, so it gets slower with every extra person.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Sharing is LAN only.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: Partial &mdash; DC++ can run on a LAN, but many public hubs route your traffic over the internet. D-LAN: Yes. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: Yes. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; cloud storage uploads your files to remote servers on the internet. Steam Local Sharing: Partial &mdash; local streaming stays on your LAN, but Steam still contacts its servers to sign you in. USB Flash Drive: Yes.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Share any file with drag and drop.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: No &mdash; DC++ shares whole folders you configure in advance, not files dropped in on the spot. D-LAN: Partial &mdash; D-LAN has supported drag-and-drop since version 1.4, but you have to navigate to its share list before you can drop files in. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: Partial &mdash; you can drop files into a shared folder, but only after the share and permissions are set up. Cloud Storage: Yes. Steam Local Sharing: No &mdash; Steam only shares games and library content, not arbitrary files. USB Flash Drive: Partial &mdash; you can drag files onto the drive, but it must be plugged in and carried to each device.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Simple UI for non-technical users.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: No &mdash; DC++ has a dense, technical interface built around hubs, slots, and download queues. D-LAN: No &mdash; D-LAN's interface assumes some comfort with networking and shared folders. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: No &mdash; SMB setup involves OS settings, permissions, and drive mapping that trip up non-technical users. Cloud Storage: Yes. Steam Local Sharing: Yes. USB Flash Drive: Yes.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>Works without the internet.</strong> LANBucket: Yes. DC++: Yes. D-LAN: Yes. LocalSend: Yes. SMB Share: Yes. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; cloud storage needs an internet connection to reach the provider's servers. Steam Local Sharing: No &mdash; Steam normally needs the internet to sign in before its features work. USB Flash Drive: Yes.{% endt %}</p>
                    <p>{% t %}<strong>On-the-fly compression.</strong> LANBucket: Yes &mdash; LANBucket automatically compresses files as they are sent and decompresses them as they arrive, in real time, which can make transfers of compressible files noticeably faster. DC++: No &mdash; DC++ transfers files without any automatic compression. D-LAN: No &mdash; D-LAN sends files as-is, with no on-the-fly compression. LocalSend: No &mdash; LocalSend transfers files without compressing them on the fly. SMB Share: No &mdash; SMB copies files byte-for-byte with no transparent compression by default. Cloud Storage: No &mdash; cloud uploads don't compress your files on the fly before transfer. Steam Local Sharing: No &mdash; Steam doesn't offer on-the-fly compression for file transfers. USB Flash Drive: No &mdash; copying to a flash drive doesn't compress your files.{% endt %}</p>
                </div>
